@meta
id: 10-18
title: The whole element
chapter: 10
type: lesson
skills: obj-tag, inner-vs-around
par: 58
minutes: 6
vscode: select from the < of the opening tag to the > of the closing one
open: OrderSummary.tsx

@brief
`at` is the element: opening tag, contents, closing tag. It is what you
want when the element itself should stop existing, or should become a
different element entirely.

Two changes from the design review:

  - the `(estimated)` note next to the delivery date goes. It is wrong
    more often than it is right and support have asked for it to be
    removed. The delivery date itself stays.
  - the hand-rolled status span is replaced by the shared component:
    `<Badge status={order.status} />`

Leave the heading alone. Watch the spacing on the delivery line — the
`<p>` should read as one sentence with no gap left behind.

@keys
| `at` | the element, tags included |
| `dat` | delete it entirely |
| `cat` | replace it with something else |
| `it` `cit` | contents only, from the last lesson |

@hints
One of these elements is being deleted and the other is being replaced by
a different element. Neither job is about the contents.
--
`dat` removes the whole element. `cat` removes it and leaves you in insert
mode where it stood, which is what you want for the swap.
--
`2f(` lands you inside the note on the delivery line. `f{` lands you
inside the span on the next one.

@solution
    7j 2f( dat
    j 0 f{ cat <Badge status={order.status} /> <Esc>
    :wq<Enter>

Fifty-one keystrokes, thirty of them the new component.

The `cat` half is the one to keep. Swapping an element for another
element is a constant job in a React codebase — a `div` becomes a
`section`, a hand-written control becomes the design system's version —
and the naive way to do it is to delete the opening tag, delete the
closing tag, and then find you have left the contents stranded in the
middle. `cat` treats the element as one thing, because it is one thing.

`dat` has a wrinkle worth meeting here rather than in a real branch. On
an inline element like the note you just removed, it does exactly what
you expect. On an element that sits alone on its own line, `dat` deletes
the element but not the line it was on, so you are left with an empty
line and its indent. That is not a bug — the object is the element, and
the line is not part of it — but it means `dat` on a block element is
usually followed by `dd`.

@start OrderSummary.tsx
import { Badge } from '../components/Badge';
import { formatDate } from '../format';

export function OrderSummary({ order }: Props) {
  return (
    <section className="order-summary">
      <h2>Order summary</h2>
      <p>Delivery {formatDate(order.eta)}<em>(estimated)</em></p>
      <span className="badge badge-status">{order.status}</span>
    </section>
  );
}

@expected OrderSummary.tsx
import { Badge } from '../components/Badge';
import { formatDate } from '../format';

export function OrderSummary({ order }: Props) {
  return (
    <section className="order-summary">
      <h2>Order summary</h2>
      <p>Delivery {formatDate(order.eta)}</p>
      <Badge status={order.status} />
    </section>
  );
}
